Great question! Here are some more details on each of the techniques you mentioned, along with sample Java code to demonstrate their impact on runtime and memory efficiency.
1. Using primitive data types instead of object wrappers
Using primitive data types instead of object wrappers can significantly reduce memory usage in Java. This is because object wrappers (such as Integer or Double) are themselves objects, which means they have additional overhead in terms of memory and processing time. By contrast, primitive data types (such as int or double) are simple values that are stored directly in memory.
Here's an example of how using primitive data types can improve performance:
```
// Summing an array of integers using object wrappers
Integer[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
   sum += nums[i];
}
// Summing an array of integers using primitive data types
int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
   sum += nums[i];
}
```
In this example, using primitive data types instead of object wrappers can save memory and improve performance.
2. Implementing lazy initialization
Lazy initialization is a technique that defers object creation until it is actually needed. This can save memory by avoiding the creation of unnecessary objects. In Java, lazy initialization is often used with singleton classes, which are classes that should only have one instance.
Here's an example of how lazy initialization can improve performance:
```
// Eagerly initialized singleton class
public class EagerSingleton {
   private static final EagerSingleton instance = new EagerSingleton();
   private EagerSingleton() {}
   public static EagerSingleton getInstance() {
      return instance;
   }
}
// Lazily initialized singleton class
public class LazySingleton {
   private static volatile LazySingleton instance;
   private LazySingleton() {}
   public static synchronized LazySingleton getInstance() {
      if (instance == null) {
         instance = new LazySingleton();
      }
      return instance;
   }
}
```
In this example, the `EagerSingleton` class is eagerly initialized, meaning that its instance is created as soon as the class is loaded. By contrast, the `LazySingleton` class is lazily initialized, meaning that its instance is only created when the `getInstance()` method is called for the first time. This can save memory by avoiding the creation of the `LazySingleton` instance until it is actually needed.
3. Using StringBuilder instead of String concatenation
String concatenation creates new String objects, whereas StringBuilder can modify a single object. This can save memory by avoiding the creation of unnecessary String objects.
Here's an example of how using StringBuilder can improve performance:
```
// Concatenating strings using String concatenation
String sentence = "";
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
   sentence += "hello";
}
// Concatenating strings using StringBuilder
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
   sb.append("hello");
}
String sentence = sb.toString();
```
In this example, using StringBuilder instead of String concatenation can save memory by avoiding the creation of unnecessary String objects.
I hope these examples help illustrate how these techniques can be used to optimize Java code performance and reduce memory usage!

