Assuming we want to allow users to sort the table by any column and filter the data based on a specific criteria, we can use the following HTML page code that includes jQuery and TableSorter plugin:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Dynamic Table with Sorting and Filtering</title>
	<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.5.1.min.js"></script>
	<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery.tablesorter/2.31.3/js/jquery.tablesorter.min.js"></script>
	<script>
		$(function() {
			$("#dynamic-table").tablesorter();
			$("#filter").on("keyup", function() {
				var value = $(this).val().toLowerCase();
				$("#dynamic-table tr").filter(function() {
					$(this).toggle($(this).text().toLowerCase().indexOf(value) > -1)
				});
			});
		});
	</script>
	<style>
		table {
			border-collapse: collapse;
			width: 100%;
		}
		th, td {
			text-align: left;
			padding: 8px;
			border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
		}
		tr:hover {
			background-color: #f5f5f5;
		}
		input[type=text] {
			width: 100%;
			padding: 12px 20px;
			margin: 8px 0;
			box-sizing: border-box;
			border: none;
			border-bottom: 2px solid #ccc;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Dynamic Table with Sorting and Filtering</h1>
	<input type="text" id="filter" placeholder="Search...">
	<table id="dynamic-table">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Units Imported</th>
				<th>Price per Unit</th>
				<th>Total Cost</th>
				<th>Tariff Revenue</th>
				<th>Domestic Production</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>0</td>
				<td>0</td>
				<td>0</td>
				<td>0</td>
				<td>0</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>100</td>
				<td>5</td>
				<td>500</td>
				<td>1000</td>
				<td>100</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>200</td>
				<td>7</td>
				<td>1400</td>
				<td>2000</td>
				<td>200</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>300</td>
				<td>10</td>
				<td>3000</td>
				<td>3000</td>
				<td>300</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
</body>
</html>
```
This HTML page includes a search bar for filtering the data and the TableSorter plugin for sorting the table by any column. Users can simply type in the search bar to filter the rows that match the search query, and click on any column header to sort the rows by that column in ascending or descending order. This interactive table provides a more user-friendly experience for analyzing the impact of tariffs on imported goods.

