The worst-case time complexity of this algorithm is O(n * m), where n is the length of the text string `str1` and m is the length of the pattern string `str2`. This occurs when the pattern string matches the last few characters of the text string, forcing the algorithm to compare each character of the pattern string with each character of the text string.
To optimize this algorithm for large strings, we can use a more efficient string matching algorithm such as the Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) algorithm or the Boyer-Moore algorithm. These algorithms use pre-processing techniques to reduce the number of character comparisons required and can achieve a worst-case time complexity of O(n + m). The KMP algorithm, for example, creates a partial match table that allows the algorithm to skip over previously matched characters in the text string. The Boyer-Moore algorithm uses a heuristic that skips over characters in the text string that cannot possibly match the pattern string.

