```
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
  var uniqueArray = [];
  var hashTable = {};
  for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    var currentValue = arr[i];
    if (!hashTable[currentValue]) {
      uniqueArray.push(currentValue);
      hashTable[currentValue] = true;
    }
  }
  return uniqueArray;
}
var arr = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 1, 4];
var uniqueArr = removeDuplicates(arr);
console.log(uniqueArr); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```
This program uses a hash table to keep track of the unique values in the array. We loop through the array and check if the current value exists in the hash table. If it doesn't, we add it to the unique array and set its value in the hash table to true. If it does exist, we skip it and move on to the next value.
This program should work for any type of data in the array, not just numerical values. If there are any irregularities in the array, such as null or undefined values, they will be treated as distinct values and included in the output.

