    forbiddenLetters := []string{"e", "u"}
    for _, letter := range forbiddenLetters {
        if strings.Contains(color, letter) {
            return true
        }
    }
    return false
}
```
The code now meets the following criteria:
1. At least one vowel in the color name.
2. No letter 'e' or 'u' in the color name.
3. The colors must be output in alphabetical order.

Identifying the poetic form of a given verse is a complex task that requires knowledge of poetic forms and patterns. However, one approach to solving this problem is to use regular expressions to identify common patterns associated with different poetic forms. Here's a C# code snippet in Markdown format that takes a verse as input and outputs its poetic form based on the identified pattern:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string verse = "The woods are lovely, dark and deep, But I have promises to keep, And miles to go before I sleep, And miles to go before I sleep.";
        string pattern = @"(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b),\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b),\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b),\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\s+(\b\w+\b)\.";
        Match match = Regex.Match(verse, pattern);
        if (match.Success)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("The verse is a quatrain.");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("The verse is not a quatrain.");
        }
    }
}
```
This code snippet uses a regular expression pattern to match a quatrain, which is a poetic form consisting of four lines. The pattern matches four lines, each containing four words separated by commas and spaces, followed by a period. If the pattern matches the input verse, the program outputs that the verse is a quatrain. Otherwise, it outputs that the verse is not a quatrain. 
Note that this is just one example of how to identify a poetic form using regular expressions. The approach and pattern used will depend on the specific poetic form you are trying to identify.

